/* ==============================================================
   01 — Your first program: getting the robot ready to move
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   What a .mxprog file is, and the commands that take a
             robot from powered-on to ready for motion
   NEEDS     Robot powered on, motors powered (RESET pressed on
             the power supply), connected in CONTROL mode.
             Nothing else — this file does the rest.
   WATCH     The arm straightens, then makes one small move
   TIME      ~15 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   /!\ BEFORE YOU PRESS RUN

   This program moves the arm to its ZERO POSITION: fully
   stretched straight out in front of the base, with the shaft
   all the way up. From wherever it was left, that can be a large
   sweep of the whole arm. Clear the area in front of and around
   the robot first.

   The MCS500's power supply has a key switch. In AUTOMATIC the
   robot runs once its motors are powered. In MANUAL it only
   moves while someone holds the enabling device halfway. In
   LOCKED it will not power its motors at all. If ActivateRobot
   below fails, that switch and the RESET button are the first
   two things to check.

   If you are not sure the poses are clear on your setup, run it
   in simulation first — deactivate the robot and send
   ActivateSim(1). See the README.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE WHOLE IDEA

   This file is a plain text file. Each line is one command. The
   robot reads them top to bottom and does them in that order.

   That is the entire programming model. There is no project
   file, no compile step, no runtime to install. What you type is
   what gets sent to the robot.

   WHAT THIS LANGUAGE DOES NOT HAVE

   No IF. No WHILE. No FOR. No jumps, no labels, no branching.

   That surprises people, so be ready for it. It is not a missing
   feature — it is the product decision that defines Mecademic.
   An MCS500 is a precision motion component, not a controller
   trying to be a PLC. The application logic — the state machine,
   the vision result, the recipe selection, the error recovery —
   lives in the customer's PC, IPC or PLC, in whatever language
   they already use. The robot is handed motion, and it executes
   motion beautifully.

   COMMENTS

   Comments are C/C++ style. This block you are reading is a
   block comment, opened with slash-star and closed with
   star-slash. A double slash comments to the end of the line.

   In the MecaPortal editor, Ctrl+/ toggles comments on whatever
   lines you have selected. Command names are case-insensitive,
   so SetWrf, SetWRF and setwrf all reach the same command — but
   pick one style and keep to it.

   UNITS

   Millimetres. Degrees. Seconds. Always, everywhere, with no
   setting to change and no way to get it wrong.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ==============================================================
   PART 1 — MAKING THE ROBOT READY FOR MOTION
   ==============================================================

   An MCS500 that has just been powered on will refuse every
   motion command you send it. Four commands fix that, and they
   have to happen in this order.

   The useful property: ALL OF THEM ARE SAFE TO RUN EVERY TIME.
   Steps 1 to 3 are idempotent — none fails if the robot is
   already in the state it asks for, each just reports that there
   was nothing to do. Step 0 is the exception and is explained
   below. So this block can sit at the top of any program without
   you having to know what state the robot was left in, which is
   exactly why every example in this repo opens with it.

   Watch the log panel while this runs. Each command answers.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* 0. DROP EVERYTHING THE LAST PROGRAM LEFT BEHIND.
      Deactivating loses every non-persistent setting: tool and
      world frames, speeds, blending, payload, torque limits,
      posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump heights.
      They all return to firmware defaults when the robot comes
      back up.
      Answers [2004] Motors deactivated.

      This is the one command here that is NOT idempotent — it
      can only run when the robot is activated, and answers with
      an error if it is not. That is harmless: on a robot fresh
      from power-on there is nothing to deactivate and nothing
      to clear, so the error is expected and the rest of the file
      continues normally.

      It does not move the arm. It does NOT reset persistent
      settings — joint limits, work zone, tool sphere, collision,
      P-Stop 2 and calibration config all survive. See
      00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those. */
DeactivateRobot()


/* 1. ENERGISE THE MOTORS.
      Activates all motors and releases the joint brakes.
      Answers [2000] Motors activated.

      Can be run in ANY robot state. If the robot is already
      activated, it answers and does nothing — which is why it is
      safe here unconditionally.

      If it answers [1013] Activation failed instead, the motors
      have no power: a stop signal is active, or nobody has
      pressed RESET since power-up or since the key switch moved. */
ActivateRobot()


/* --- WHERE IS Home()? ------------------------------------------
      If you have used a Meca500, you are waiting for a homing
      step here. There is none. The MCS500 has high-accuracy
      ABSOLUTE encoders: it knows where every joint is from the
      moment it powers up, so an activated MCS500 is immediately
      ready for motion.

      The robot will accept a Home() command, and ignore it — the
      manual says it has effect only on the Meca500. Leave it out
      rather than teach a step that does nothing.

      The one rule the absolute encoders impose: never turn the
      shaft (joint 4) by hand beyond plus or minus ten turns, for
      example with the brakes released. That is the range the
      encoder can count. */


/* 2. CLEAR ANY FAULT.
      Acknowledges and clears an error state left over from a
      previous run — a collision, a work zone breach, a torque
      limit trip.
      Answers [2005] if there was an error to reset, or [2006]
      if there was not. Both are fine.

      ORDER MATTERS HERE. ResetError can only run once the robot
      is "ready for motion", which on the MCS500 simply means
      activated. So it goes after ActivateRobot, not before. A
      robot that errors while DEACTIVATED never enters an error
      state at all and needs no reset.

      Note that clearing a fault does not resume motion — that is
      the next command, and it is the step people forget. */
ResetError()


/* 3. RELEASE THE MOTION QUEUE.
      Resumes movement if it was paused: by ResetError above, by
      a PauseMotion command, by a torque overload configured in
      pause-motion mode, or by a P-Stop 2 signal that has since
      cleared.
      Answers [2043] Motion resumed.

      Leaving this out is the classic "I cleared the error and it
      still will not move" problem. */
ResumeMotion()


/* --- The robot is now ready for motion -------------------------
   From here on, motion commands will be accepted.

   In the MecaPortal you would normally do all of the above with
   the buttons in the toolbar. They are written out here because
   a host program has to send exactly these, in exactly this
   order, and because seeing them makes the robot's state model
   concrete.

   WHAT THIS DOES *NOT* DO

   Ready for motion is not the same as predictable. These
   commands (after Deactivate) do not clear anything persistent
   the previous user left behind — not a restricted joint limit,
   not a work zone, not simulation mode — and they do not touch
   the time-scaling slider either.

   Deactivate only wipes every non-persistent setting in one
   stroke.

   If you are on a robot you do not know, and you want the demo
   to behave the way it behaved when you rehearsed it, run
   00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog instead of this file.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ==============================================================
   PART 2 — PUTTING THE SETTINGS IN A KNOWN STATE
   ============================================================== */

/* Being ready to move is not the same as being ready to run YOUR
   program. Speeds, reference frames, blending and posture are
   all still whatever the last program left behind.

   That is what the init helper is for. One line, and every
   setting this repo cares about is explicit:

       StartProgram("00_common/01_init")

   It is commented out below because StartProgram only finds
   programs already SAVED ON THE ROBOT. Save
   00_common/01_init.mxprog as "00_common/01_init" first, then
   uncomment. Read 00_common/01_init.mxprog — it is the most
   reusable thing in this repo. */

// StartProgram("00_common/01_init")

// Until you have saved it, set the two that matter here:
SetJointVel(15)
SetBlending(100)


/* ==============================================================
   PART 3 — A FIRST MOVE
   ============================================================== */

// Move to the zero position: all four joints at zero. The arm
// points straight out in front of the base, fully stretched,
// with the shaft all the way up. It is the position the
// MecaPortal's "Zero all joints" button goes to, and the one the
// manual suggests parking in before you power off.
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

// Rotate joint 1 by 30 degrees. The whole arm swings about the
// base. Nothing else moves, because none of the other three
// numbers changed.
MoveJoints(30, 0, 0, 0)

// Back to where we started.
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   ONE THING THAT IS NOT OBVIOUS: THE MOTION QUEUE

   The readiness commands in Part 1 are ROBOT CONTROL commands:
   they execute immediately, the moment the robot receives them.

   The three moves in Part 3 are MOTION commands: they go into a
   MOTION QUEUE and are worked through in order, first in first
   out.

   So "the program finished" and "the robot stopped moving" are
   two different moments. All three moves can be sitting in the
   robot before the arm has finished the first one.

   This matters as soon as you coordinate with anything outside
   the robot — a vacuum cup, a conveyor, a camera. The tool for
   that is SetCheckpoint(), covered in
   07_delay_and_sequencing.mxprog.

   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Select just the line MoveJoints(30, 0, 0, 0) and press
      Ctrl+Enter. Only that line runs. This is how to demo a
      single command without running the whole file, and it is
      one feature that makes the MecaPortal genuinely useful.

   2. Run the whole file twice in a row and watch the log panel.
      The second time, ResetError answers [2006] — there was no
      error to reset — and does nothing. No failure, no special
      case. That is what "safe to run every time" looks like.

   3. Force an error to see step 2 work: run
      MoveJoints(0, 0, 10, 0), which asks joint 3 — the shaft —
      to rise 10 mm above its top stop. Its range is -102 to 0
      mm. The robot refuses and goes into error. Now run this
      file again — ResetError clears it and ResumeMotion releases
      the queue, with no button pressing.

   NEXT   02_move_joints.mxprog — what those four numbers are.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
